{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"c6d1768263f2de14","name":"card_event_rasputitsa","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_rasputitsa","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"RASPUTITSA","text":"Each unit is dealt damage equal to its operation cost.","flavorText":"Rasputitsa, literally the “season of bad roads”, describes the periods of deep mud that k strikes Russia, Ukraine and Bielorussia during the spring thaw and autumn rains. During WWII, melting snow, heavy rainfall and waterlogged soils could turn unpaved roads and open ground into near impassable swamps, trapping trucks, artillery and even tanks. Rasputitsa became an important feature of fighting on the Eastern Front. In the autumn of 1941, it severely hindered movement during Operation Typhoon, the German offensive toward Moscow. Vehicles became bogged down and supply routes deteriorated as the Wehrmacht advanced farther into Soviet territory. The mud was not purely a Soviet advantage, however, as Red Army movements and logistics suffered as well. Rather than single-handedly stopping the German advance, rasputitsa compounded existing problems of Soviet resistance, enormous distances to cover, and overstretched supply lines.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_rasputitsa","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_rasputitsa_title","SourceString":"RASPUTITSA","LocalizedString":"RASPUTITSA"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_rasputitsa_text","SourceString":"Each unit is dealt damage equal to its operation cost.","LocalizedString":"Each unit is dealt damage equal to its operation cost."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_rasputitsa_history","SourceString":"Rasputitsa, literally the “season of bad roads”, describes the periods of deep mud that k strikes Russia, Ukraine and Bielorussia during the spring thaw and autumn rains. During WWII, melting snow, heavy rainfall and waterlogged soils could turn unpaved roads and open ground into near impassable swamps, trapping trucks, artillery and even tanks. Rasputitsa became an important feature of fighting on the Eastern Front. In the autumn of 1941, it severely hindered movement during Operation Typhoon, the German offensive toward Moscow. Vehicles became bogged down and supply routes deteriorated as the Wehrmacht advanced farther into Soviet territory. The mud was not purely a Soviet advantage, however, as Red Army movements and logistics suffered as well. Rather than single-handedly stopping the German advance, rasputitsa compounded existing problems of Soviet resistance, enormous distances to cover, and overstretched supply lines.","LocalizedString":"Rasputitsa, literally the “season of bad roads”, describes the periods of deep mud that k strikes Russia, Ukraine and Bielorussia during the spring thaw and autumn rains. During WWII, melting snow, heavy rainfall and waterlogged soils could turn unpaved roads and open ground into near impassable swamps, trapping trucks, artillery and even tanks. Rasputitsa became an important feature of fighting on the Eastern Front. In the autumn of 1941, it severely hindered movement during Operation Typhoon, the German offensive toward Moscow. Vehicles became bogged down and supply routes deteriorated as the Wehrmacht advanced farther into Soviet territory. The mud was not purely a Soviet advantage, however, as Red Army movements and logistics suffered as well. Rather than single-handedly stopping the German advance, rasputitsa compounded existing problems of Soviet resistance, enormous distances to cover, and overstretched supply lines."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Patriotic_Push'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/A